 [17592186423620 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423620 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423620 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423620 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the matrix \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(V).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423620 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423620 #edb/ref 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435337 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435337 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435337 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435337 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435337 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435337 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175